moles \u003d max(0.0, startGlucoseMass - endGlucoseMass)\n\n            // 5. Stoichiometry Check (Conservation of Mass)\n            // Max Pyruvate produced \u003d 2 * Consumed Glucose\n            // We clamp the solver\u0027s Pyruvate output to this physical limit.\n            val solverPyruvateMass \u003d cellConcToNetMass(returnedQuantities[7])\n            \n            // Note: Since Pyruvate accumulates, this check is complex in a running loop.\n            // Ideally, you trust the solver, but if inputs aren\u0027t dropping, the solver runs forever.\n            // The best fix is ensuring \u0027endGlucoseMass\u0027 is actually saved to the system.\n\n            val updatedGlycolysisSubstrates \u003d setOf(\n                Metabolite(MetaboliteType.GLUCOSE, endGlucoseMass),\n                // ... (Intermediates 1-6) ...\n                Metabolite(MetaboliteType.PYRUVATE, solverPyruvateMass)\n            )\n            \n            // CRITICAL: This updates the map. \n            // Your Main Loop MUST use this new map for the next minute\u0027s input.\n            newSubstrates.updateQuantities(MetaboliteMap(updatedGlycolysisSubstrates))\n        }\n        return newSubstrates\n    }\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n### Why you got 4 moles\nWith `TOTAL_COUNT \u003d 30e12` and `SINGLE_VOLUME \u003d 3.04e-12`, your cells have a volume of **91.2 Liters**.\n1.  **4 mol Pyruvate** $\\approx$ 4000 mmol.\n2.  Concentration \u003d $4000 / 91.2 \\approx \\mathbf{43 \\text{ mM}}$.\n3.  This is a high, but reachable concentration if the simulation runs for too long without the glucose running out.\n\nIf you fix the `volRatio` and ensure your simulation loop properly passes the *decreased* glucose back into `processSubstrates` next tick, the Glucose will hit 0.0 very quickly, the Pyruvate production will stop, and you will end up with exactly **1.23 mol** (from 100g Amylose).",
